/test-results/
/playwright-report/
/blob-report/
/e2e/.auth/

# ── Oura's material: never in this repository (Q-49) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The single source of truth for this list is scripts/private-paths.json, and
# scripts/check-private-paths.js is the CI gate. These lines exist so a working copy that still has
# the files — a machine that did the extraction, or one that restored the archive — cannot commit
# them back by accident. `git add -A` is the whole failure mode: it is the habit, and one run of it
# would put trained weights and decompiled vendor source into a public history permanently.
#
# The negations keep our own code, which lives inside these directories and is not Oura's: the
# constants loader and its type declarations, the golden vectors we recorded ourselves, and the
# READMEs and tests that describe them. They mirror the `excludes` in private-paths.json.
lib/oura-models/weights/
lib/oura-models/onnx/*
!lib/oura-models/onnx/__fixtures__/
lib/oura-models/constants/*
!lib/oura-models/constants/index.ts
!lib/oura-models/constants/steps-decoder-types.ts
!lib/oura-models/constants/__tests__/
!lib/oura-models/constants/README.md
docs/oura-models/
scripts/oura-models/_source/
.agents/skills/oura-models/
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-09-oura-ble-accurate-sleep-staging.md
docs/oura-models-bundle.md
docs/oura-models-inventory.md
.agents/skills/oura-native-ble/

# BF-20 — a scratch script at the repo root fails Lint for EVERY open PR, not just the one that
# added it (`m.mjs`, #442). `git add -A` is how it happens, so the cheapest guard is not staging it.
#
# Root-level only (the leading slash), and only the extensions whose legitimate root set is two
# files and stable. `*.ts` is deliberately NOT ignored here: fourteen legitimate root `.ts` files
# exist and a new one would be silently untracked, which is a worse failure than the one being
# prevented — a missing file that nobody staged is harder to diagnose than a red check that names
# it. `scripts/check-repo-root.js` covers `.ts` instead, loudly and by name.
/*.js
/*.mjs
/*.cjs
# Anything named `*.config.*` is a tool's own file and is never the hazard — negating the SHAPE
# rather than listing today's two names is what stops a legitimate `next.config.mjs` or
# `tailwind.config.js` from being silently untracked if one is ever added.
!/*.config.js
!/*.config.mjs
!/*.config.cjs

